# serial_cycle -- a ring: three domains, each asked by one neighbor and
# asking the other, with no end anywhere in it.
#
# Every declaration in this arc has had ends. serial_duplex gave a pair two
# directions; serial_relay gave a chain a middle, and its two ends were the
# whole point -- a client that only asks, a backend that only answers, and
# one domain between them holding both roles.
#
# This declaration removes the ends. `alder` writes `ask_ab` and reads
# `ask_ca`; `birch` writes `ask_bc` and reads `ask_ab`; `cedar` writes
# `ask_ca` and reads `ask_bc`. Every domain is a middle, so the flow graph
# closes on itself and an ask placed by alder can travel a full lap and
# arrive back at the domain that wrote it.
#
# Read the grants and two facts fall out without a role word anywhere.
# Every domain spans exactly two peers, so nothing here is an end. And
# exactly one directed walk visits all three domains once and returns to
# where it began -- alder to birch to cedar to alder -- which is the
# circulation order, found by arithmetic rather than declared.
#
# `taken` is alder's own tail, the one number about a completed lap that
# alder alone may write. Its reader is cedar, alder's predecessor, so the
# tail stays a one-way street like every other region here.
#
# The ring's honest cost is what it takes away: no domain holds a grant on
# every region, so no domain can total the lap it is part of. alder reads
# three of four regions and cedar's segment is closed to it. A ring has no
# vantage point, and only the parent outside it sees the whole.
#
# Read whole by `caravan/read.rye`; exercised by `caravan/cycle.rye`.

system serial_cycle

domain alder
domain birch
domain cedar

channel alder birch
channel birch cedar
channel cedar alder

region ask_ab 4096
region ask_bc 4096
region ask_ca 4096
region taken 64

grant ask_ab alder rw
grant ask_ab birch r
grant ask_bc birch rw
grant ask_bc cedar r
grant ask_ca cedar rw
grant ask_ca alder r
grant taken alder rw
grant taken cedar r
